## Original Content
Investors will buy your life insurance policy today — and collect the payout when you die.

## Summary
The AIDS crisis spurred the creation of a multibillion-dollar market where investors buy life insurance policies from individuals and collect the payout upon their death. This practice, known as death-speculation, involves financial speculation on mortality and life expectancy.

## Key Points
- The AIDS crisis catalyzed the development of a death-speculation market.
- Investors purchase life insurance policies from individuals for speculative financial gains.
- The market is valued in the billions of dollars.
- The mechanism involves collecting death benefits after the policyholder dies.


